    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d172">

                <pb facs="0283" n="167" xml:id="pg167"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.21/266.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Colombian
                    Minister</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-07-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 19, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have honor to invite your attention to
                    paragraph five<anchor n="17" xml:id="d172-f1"/> of the proposed treaty between
                    the United States and Colombia, which is now pending ratification in the Senate
                    of the United States. It has been brought to my attention that this paragraph
                    five might be interpreted to require the Panama Railroad, in order to transport
                    the specified products of Colombia, to keep the railroad open and retain
                    rolling-stock especially for this purpose, which under the ordinary
                    circumstances of traffic it would not be required to do. As you are aware, Mr.
                    Minister, it was not the intention of the two Governments to impose any burdens
                    of this character on the Panama Railroad; and therefore, in order to prevent the
                    delay which would arise from amending the treaty, I suggest that your Government
                    agree to the interpretation of this paragraph five in the sense which I have
                    indicated above. A note from you to this effect will be sufficient.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
